:question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Which of the following is a basis of the eigenspace corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 3$?"] [17592186124942 :question/answer-fields 17592186122674] [17592186124925 :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}, v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vectors $v_{1}, v_{2}$ shown above.\nIf $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ are two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 1$, what is the value of $a + b$?"] [17592186124941 :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. What are the dimensions of the eigenspaces $V_{-6}$ and $V_{2}$ corresponding to eigenvalues $λ =-6$ and $λ = 2$ of $A$, respectively?"] [17592186124941 :question/answer-fields 17592186122668] [17592186124251 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a basis of the eigenspace corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-4$."] [17592186124251 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-4\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} - (-4)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{-5x_{2} + 8x_{3} = 0; 3x_{3} = 0; 3x_{3} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-1)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and the $3$rd). Thus, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_3=0$. Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_2=0$.\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required basis is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124252 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -15 & 14 & -9 \\\\ -5 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a basis of the eigenspace corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2$?"] [17592186124252 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2$. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -15 & 14 & -9 \\\\ -5 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} - 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -15 & 12 & -9 \\\\ -5 & 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{-10x_{1} + 8x_{2} - 6x_{3} = 0; - 15x_{1} + 12x_{2} - 9x_{3} = 0; - 5x_{1} + 4x_{2} - 3x_{3} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 8 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -15 & 12 & -9 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 4 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{2}R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -15 & 12 & -9 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 4 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + 3R_{1} \\\\\n&= R_{3}:=R_{3} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ and $x_3$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac 4 5x_2-\\dfrac35x_3\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5}x_{2} - \\frac{3}{5}x_{3} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required basis is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124254 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -8 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2$ shown above.\nIf $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$, what is the value of $a+b$?"] [17592186124254 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =4$. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -8 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} - 4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -12 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{3x_{1} - 6x_{2} + 5x_{3} = 0; 6x_{1} - 12x_{2} + 10x_{3} = 0; 0 = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -12 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ and $x_3$ are free variables. On the other hand, from the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1=2x_2-\\dfrac{5}{3}x_3\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2x_{2} - \\frac{5}{3}x_{3} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe now deduce the eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2\\mathbin$:\n\n- Since $\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ we must have that $x_2=1$ and $x_3=0$. As a result, $a=2(1)-\\dfrac 53(0)=2$.\n- Since $\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ we must have that $x_2=0$ and $x_3=3$. As a result, $b=2(0)-\\dfrac53(3)=-5$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b=2+(-5)=-3\n$$"] [17592186124253 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. What are the dimensions of the eigenspaces $V_{0}$ and $V_{-2}$ corresponding to eigenvalues $\\lambda=0$ and $\\lambda=-2$ of $A$, respectively?"] [17592186124253 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A - λI) = 0 \\\\\n&= \\begin{vmatrix}1 - λ & 6\\end{vmatrix}- 3 \\\\\n0\\begin{vmatrix}-2 - λ & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}- 3 - λ &= 0 \\\\\n(-2 - λ) \\mid 1 - λ\\begin{vmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}- 3 - λ &= 0 \\\\\n&= (-2 - λ)((1 - λ)(-3 - λ) + 3) = 0 \\\\\n&= - (λ + 2)(λ^{2} + 2λ) = 0 \\\\\n&= λ(λ + 2)^{2} = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda_1=0$ (simple root) and\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\lambda_3=-2\n$$\n\n(double root).\n\nLet's now consider the eigenspaces corresponding to each eigenvector:\n\n- First, we consider $\\lambda=0$. Since $V_{0}$ corresponds to a simple root, we obtain\n$\\textrm{dim}(V_{0})=1$.\n- Then, we consider $\\lambda=-2$, which is a repeated root of the characteristic equation. To find the dimension of $V_{-2}$, we row-reduce the matrix $(A - (-2) I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$:\n$A - λI|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} - (-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3} \\mid:=R_{3} + (-\\frac{1}{3})R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). This implies that we will have $3-1=2$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = -2$. Therefore,\n$\\textrm{dim}(V_{-2})=2$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186122573 17592186122574 17592186122575 17592186122576 17592186122577 17592186122669 17592186122670 17592186122671 17592186122672 17592186122673 17592186122675 17592186122676 17592186122677 17592186122678 17592186122679 17592186122999 17592186123000 17592186123001 17592186123002 17592186123003] "immutable_fields" [[17592186122572 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122572 :answer-field/id] [17592186122572 :answer-field/key] [17592186122572 :answer-field/type] [17592186122668 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122668 :answer-field/id] [17592186122668 :answer-field/key] [17592186122668 :answer-field/type] [17592186122674 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122674 :answer-field/id] [17592186122674 :answer-field/key] [17592186122674 :answer-field/type] [17592186122998 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122998 :answer-field/id] [17592186122998 :answer-field/key] [17592186122998 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
